Great.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.919
All right, so what
is batch computing?

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.759
I think we're all kind
of familiar with what

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.170
batch computing is,
but really the way

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.490
we define it is, for the purpose
of this talk and in general,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.948
it's really any
kind of computation

00:02:15.948 --> 00:02:17.450
that's done offline.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.946
This is as opposed to, let's
say, online computation

00:02:19.946 --> 00:02:21.320
like a website or
an application,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.090
where there's a
user playing with it

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.100
and they expect
immediate responses.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.860
Batch computing is,
I have a binary.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.370
I have some amount
of data, I say,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.960
hey, computers, take this
binary, run out on this data,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.272
give me the results and
I'll come back to it later.

00:02:35.272 --> 00:02:36.980
Some interesting
examples are, of course,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.070
rendering, as in the case
of movies or other images.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.730
Transcoding, either videos
or audio or images from one

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.717
format to another,
maybe reshaping it etc.

00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:48.800
Finance and accounting,
engineering and scientific

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.170
simulations, of course,
classic data analysis

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.960
is, of course, just
another form of batch.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.230
And there's lots of
different modalities.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.064
There's lots of different
ways people do it.

00:02:56.064 --> 00:02:58.438
There's embarrassingly parallel,
or maybe high throughput

00:02:58.438 --> 00:02:59.780
computing is sometimes called.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.180
There's always tightly coupled
high performance computing

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.440
or MPI.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.680
Those are all terms used for it.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.760
OK, let's talk a little
bit about Preemptible VMs.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.257
So what is a Preemptible VM?

00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:13.840
Well, let's start
with the good parts.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.464
First of all, they're
incredibly cheap.

00:03:15.464 --> 00:03:19.160
They're up to 80% cheaper than
a regular GCE virtual machine.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.660
They cost around
$0.01 per core hour.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.119
They're very easy to use.

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:25.410
In fact, if you guys are
familiar with our GCloud tool,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.330
it's Gcloud, Compute, Instances,
Create, then dash dash

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.492
preemptible, and just hit Enter.

00:03:30.492 --> 00:03:32.700
To create a regular VM, you
just remove the dash dash

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.500
preemptible, and you've
got a regular VM.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.639
Many of our customers are
actually using them for VL.

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:41.430
This isn't some sort
of like excess compute

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.554
that's just sitting on the
side and that we're sort

00:03:43.554 --> 00:03:44.910
of giving away as fire prices.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.070
We have clusters, and
you'll hear from Bert,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:50.850
we have clusters that
are over 10,000 cores.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.480
But some things to keep in mind.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.570
One, same great VM, same great
options, same great networking,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.162
same images et cetera.

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:02.370
Almost every feature that's
available for regular VMs

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.661
are available for preemptibles.

00:04:03.661 --> 00:04:06.195
But, we can and do preempt them.

00:04:06.195 --> 00:04:07.820
That's why they're
called preemptibles,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.028
it's right there in the name.

00:04:09.028 --> 00:04:10.070
You get what you pay for.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.111
We give you-- if we do
decide to preempt your VM,

00:04:12.111 --> 00:04:13.890
we'll give you 30
seconds worth of notice.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.870
And then we will send
a shutdown signal.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.120
You can use a shutdown
script, as usual,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.490
to handle the shutdown event,
and then back up your data

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.190
or do what you need, but you
get 30 seconds of notice.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.790
The other caveat is we only
give you 24 hours of up time.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.920
If your VM is not
preempted in the 24 hours,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.170
then we just shut
it down for you.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.570
And of course, there's no
SLAs or guarantees because,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.653
when we can take the thing
away, what kind of SLAs

00:04:35.653 --> 00:04:36.479
can we give you?

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:38.520
All right, so usually when
I tell this to people,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.020
they're like, OK, that's
just sounds crazy, right?

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.580
Like, you're going to
give me a virtual machine.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.497
I'm going to pay you
relatively little for it,

00:04:43.497 --> 00:04:45.454
but you're going to just
take it away whenever.

00:04:45.454 --> 00:04:47.260
Like, what can I
possibly do with it?

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.750
Well, the idea is that
you're not doing something

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.791
with one virtual machine.

00:04:49.791 --> 00:04:51.210
You probably have a few of them.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.980
And you have some workload.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.870
And the best place where
these are applicable

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.430
is when you have a workload that
has a lot of individual units

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.860
that are independent and small
relative to the workload.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.370
So let's take the example
of media transcoding.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.260
Let's say you have a
few million images that

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.460
might be coming
from a satellite,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.876
or being uploaded
to some website,

00:05:12.876 --> 00:05:14.790
or something,
something, something.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.650
And then what you want to do is,
you want take all these images

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.930
and you want to transcode
them into some other format.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.638
Maybe you want to
rescale them, maybe you

00:05:20.638 --> 00:05:22.784
want to greyscale
them, whatever it is.

00:05:22.784 --> 00:05:25.200
What you'll do is, you'll put
these images in some storage

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:25.750
location.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:26.550
And we'll talk
about your options

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.910
for that in a little bit.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.960
You will have a master node that
is definitely not preemptable.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.920
You'll use a master node
that as a standard VM,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.806
because you generally don't want
your master node going away.

00:05:37.806 --> 00:05:39.180
I haven't seen
any scenario where

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.547
anybody's used the preemptible
for the master node yet.

00:05:41.547 --> 00:05:44.130
And then you'll spin up a whole
bunch of preemptible machines.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.796
You will take images out of that
storage location one at a time.

00:05:46.796 --> 00:05:49.080
You'll assign them to
the preemptible machines.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.420
And they'll start actually
running the code that you

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:52.652
have on those machines.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:54.360
And they will actually
do the computation

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.230
for you, the transcoding
of the images.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.720
Of course, if one of your
machines does get preempted--

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.855
and they will get preempted--

00:06:02.855 --> 00:06:04.230
you haven't really
lost anything.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.563
All you've lost is the work
to transcode that one image.

00:06:06.563 --> 00:06:09.205
All you will do is, your master
node will realize, oh, hey,

00:06:09.205 --> 00:06:10.080
we lost this machine.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.790
This image never got transcoded.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:12.930
So I'm just going
to queue it back up

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.638
and assign it to a
different preemptible,

00:06:14.638 --> 00:06:17.052
or maybe to another
[INAUDIBLE] machine and so on.

00:06:17.052 --> 00:06:18.510
So that's a pretty
common use case.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.680
There's obviously others.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.060
There's satellite
image analysis.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.185
There's rendering, like I
mentioned, data analysis,

00:06:23.185 --> 00:06:25.350
genomics, financial analysis.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:26.790
And there are some
things you can

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.610
type in your favorite
search engine

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:34.090
to get some articles
and blog posts.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:34.660
Cool.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.680
All right, so let's actually
talk about data and storage.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.640
As you guys know, on GCP we
have a bewildering amount

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.950
of storage options for you.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.020
In fact, what's actually
missing from the slide

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.170
is the fact that we
now have Spanner today.

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.287
And today I'm going to
talk about two primarily.

00:06:50.287 --> 00:06:52.370
I'm going to talk about
our object storage options

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.080
and block storage options.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.660
That's persistent disk
and Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:58.160
Of course, you will
still use things

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.770
like relational databases or
NoSQL databases in your batch

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.021
scenarios.

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:03.770
For example, if you're
transcoding images,

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.920
again, you might want to say
for this image the target

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.010
resolution is x, the
target color depth is y.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.010
There's some metadata
associated with that image

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.720
that you want stored
and persisted.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.386
Or perhaps you have
different priorities

00:07:16.386 --> 00:07:18.660
for the jobs et cetera.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:22.000
Cool, so let's talk
about block storage.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Just to be a little bit
clearer, block storage--

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.500
think about block storage as
the regular hard drive attached

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.880
to your PC.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.530
And we have three
primary options.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.759
We have local SSD,
persistent disk--

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:35.550
and we have two flavors
of persistent disk,

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:39.810
SSD persistent disk and
hard drive persistent disk.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.180
Let's start on the far
left with local SSD.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.920
Local SSD is our fastest option.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.253
So if you have a batch job
that's constantly referencing

00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:48.104
files, or it's working
with a specific file

00:07:48.104 --> 00:07:49.770
over and over and
over again, or it just

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.880
really needs low latency, you're
going to go with local SSD.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.894
Like the name
implies, it's actually

00:07:55.894 --> 00:07:57.810
physically attached to
the VM, to the hardware

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.960
that your VM is running on.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.170
That's why it's local.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.090
On the flip side,
it's ephemeral,

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.580
which means that once
your VM is terminated,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.780
your local SSD just goes away.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.587
It's really good for, like
I said, scratch space,

00:08:12.587 --> 00:08:14.670
when you're actually working
with the data locally

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.480
and you need it to be hot.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:19.899
Scientific workloads, especially
if you're using something

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:21.690
like GPU instances and
you just really want

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.990
to chew through data quickly.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:25.910
It has great IOS performance.

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:28.550
Of course, it is the priciest
of the three options.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.680
And you can get up to
three terabytes of it.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.643
Moving to the middle column,
you have persistent disk SSD.

00:08:33.643 --> 00:08:35.809
And after that, you have
persistent disk hard drive.

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:37.820
Unlike local SSD,
persistent disks

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.479
are, like the name
implies, persistent.

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:41.520
As you can see, we're very
clear with our naming.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.561
Preemptible VMs are
preemptible, persistent disks

00:08:43.561 --> 00:08:44.219
are persistent.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:45.260
You get what you pay for.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.180
If you need a persistent
disk that's fast,

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.680
you'll go with persistent SSD.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.680
It's a little more pricey than
persistent disk hard drive,

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:57.560
but you get great
IOPS performance.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.510
Both of these use 64--

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:02.864
up to 64 terabytes of space.

00:09:02.864 --> 00:09:05.030
Everything supports encryptions
and persistent disks

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:06.910
also support snapshotting.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.150
All right, so those-- that's
disk and block storage.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.550
And then we have, of course,
Google Cloud Storage,

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.580
which offers object
storage in the cloud.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.350
And we have four
offerings that I'm not

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.684
going to go into great depth
on because I don't really--

00:09:21.684 --> 00:09:23.766
I recommend you guys go
to the Cloud Storage talk.

00:09:23.766 --> 00:09:25.850
If you missed it, I think
it was actually earlier,

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:28.182
then I recommend you guys
check it out, record it online

00:09:28.182 --> 00:09:29.610
or just check out our website.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.270
But suffice it to say
that we have four options.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.975
Coldline is the
sort of lowest SLA,

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:37.350
and the assumption
is that you're

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:40.039
accessing data on a rare
occasion when you need it.

00:09:40.039 --> 00:09:41.580
So this is like for
archival storage.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.288
And then we have multi
regional offerings

00:09:43.288 --> 00:09:46.920
that have very high SLAs,
and they are georedundant,

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.980
so you can access your data
from a number of Google Cloud

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:53.390
regions around the world.

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.810
But how is this different
than persistent disk?

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:59.530
So, latency is higher.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.930
On the flip side, you
can store a lot of data.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.920
It just scales to exabytes,
whereas persistent disk,

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.604
as I mentioned before,
scales to 64 terabytes.

00:10:09.604 --> 00:10:11.770
Unlike persistent disk,
it's available from anywhere

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:12.760
in the world.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.330
You can use the REST
interface over HTTP,

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.815
just to request
the files you need.

00:10:17.815 --> 00:10:19.810
Persistent disk has to
be attached to GCE VM.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.476
Obviously, you can
expose it from there,

00:10:21.476 --> 00:10:25.440
but inherently it's
attached to a GCE VM.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.216
It's higher latency,
like I mentioned,

00:10:27.216 --> 00:10:28.840
and of course this
isn't blocks storage

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.010
so you can't append to a file,
you can't read specific blocks,

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.670
you can just insert, overwrite,
read entire files at a time.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.880
It offers versioning,
and of course is cheaper.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:42.580
So what I'm hoping that you
guys get out of this is,

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.100
there is a continuum of
storage options that you have.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.130
On the one extreme, you
have Coldline, and then

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.140
the other extreme,
you have local SSD.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.900
And what you probably want to
do is, you want to pick notches

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.466
along that continuum
and say, hey

00:10:55.466 --> 00:10:56.965
for my perform--
for my application,

00:10:56.965 --> 00:10:58.423
when I'm actually
computing, I need

00:10:58.423 --> 00:11:03.345
to be in, perhaps, a local SSD,
or perhaps persistent disk SSD.

00:11:03.345 --> 00:11:05.470
And then when maybe I'm
not actually using my data,

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.730
or for the data I'm not
using, or using very rarely,

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.438
I'll put into Cloud
Storage or maybe I'll

00:11:09.438 --> 00:11:14.580
put it into Coldline, and so on.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.960
Let's talk about actual
batch architectures.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:22.650
So these are samples of
what we've seen customers

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:25.380
building on Google Cloud.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:29.220
This is a very basic
starter batch architecture.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:33.220
Over here, you have our
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:34.930
One of them stores
the input data.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:37.410
So if, again, we're going
back to the running story

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.627
through this talk,
is image transcoding.

00:11:39.627 --> 00:11:42.210
If you have a bunch of images,
you might put your input images

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.640
into the input bucket.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.152
Over here, you'll have
some analyst, or some--

00:11:47.152 --> 00:11:48.610
maybe it's not a
human being, maybe

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:52.400
it's a machine that's creating
the images and putting them in.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.574
And it's putting them
into the input bucket.

00:11:54.574 --> 00:11:56.490
You'll have a head node
that is definitely not

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:57.570
running a preemptible VM.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.940
And it's running some software
like HTCondor or Slurm,

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.779
or maybe custom
scripts that you wrote.

00:12:02.779 --> 00:12:04.320
And it's spinning
up virtual machines

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.982
to go out and talk to-- to read
data from the input buckets,

00:12:07.982 --> 00:12:10.440
do the actual computation, the
transcoding or the analysis,

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:11.880
whatever it is that
you want to do.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.671
And then it actually
writes the output data

00:12:13.671 --> 00:12:16.090
to the output bucket.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.090
So let's say you've been
doing that for a while,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:19.200
and things are going well.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.270
And then you realize, all
right, well, this is--

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:22.530
I'm sort of waiting
for these files

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.000
to come down from
Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.210
and it's taking some time.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:26.700
I want something that's a
little faster, or a little lower

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:27.759
latency.

00:12:27.759 --> 00:12:29.550
What you would probably
do in that case is,

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.130
you would take the existing
start up batch architecture,

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:35.270
and you would add to it a
bunch of persistent disks.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:36.840
So you create a
parallel file system

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.840
with some compute nodes and
some persistent disks that

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.960
allow you to
basically, effectively

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:46.500
cache the data from Google Cloud
Storage closer to the actual

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.350
compute instances for the
moment when you're actually

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.440
doing the computation.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:52.940
And of course, you
can use a variety

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.910
of options, like NFS, or
[INAUDIBLE], or OrangeFS

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:56.420
and so on.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.652
Moving on from there, at
some point you'll probably

00:13:01.652 --> 00:13:03.110
realize, like, oh
wait, my workload

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:04.443
is like embarrassingly parallel.

00:13:04.443 --> 00:13:08.810
And if I lose work, if I lose a
machine, that's perfectly fine.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:12.704
Why am I not saving 80%
by using preemptible VMs?

00:13:12.704 --> 00:13:14.120
And so what you'll
probably do is,

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:15.930
you'll create two
pools of workers.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.320
One of them will be
running preemptible VMs,

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:20.719
and the other one will
be running regular nodes.

00:13:20.719 --> 00:13:22.760
It's generally advisable
that you guys don't just

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:23.930
run 100% preemptibles.

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:25.930
It's kind of tempting,
that's 80% off, I get it.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.420
But depending on your
business need and how much

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.096
progress you need
to make, you need

00:13:31.096 --> 00:13:32.720
to make a call about
what the split is.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:36.590
I've seen people do it 60-40,
50-50, 40-60, 30-70, and so on.

00:13:39.504 --> 00:13:41.170
The thing, of course,
is the preemptible

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:42.004
VMs are preemptible.

00:13:42.004 --> 00:13:44.128
We can take them away, and
sometimes we have a need

00:13:44.128 --> 00:13:45.690
to take away a lot of them.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.554
And so you want to be guaranteed
that your jobs don't crawl

00:13:48.554 --> 00:13:50.720
to a halt and your progress
doesn't crawl to a halt.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:53.010
And so it's a good idea to,
depending on your needs,

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:57.440
to have some number
of standard VMs.

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.980
And beyond that, you might go
out and grow your application

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.060
even more.

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.460
So in this example, we're adding
auto scaling and health checks.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:09.019
And I'll talk a little bit
about how that works on GCE.

00:14:09.019 --> 00:14:10.560
And this lets you
basically say, hey,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:15.034
if there's something
going on in my queue,

00:14:15.034 --> 00:14:16.700
if there's nothing
in there because it's

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:18.650
middle of the night or
maybe it's a long weekend

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.210
and all the analysts went
home and nobody's submitting

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.751
anything, just turn off all the
machines because why should I

00:14:22.751 --> 00:14:23.600
be paying for it?

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.891
Health checks basically say,
hey, if one of my machines

00:14:25.891 --> 00:14:28.070
is funky or it's not
responding, just kill the thing

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:29.360
and start it over again.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.710
Or if it got preempted,
create a new one.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.700
Of course, we have the
file system as before.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:37.450
And we're still using
Google Cloud Storage

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:38.780
for the ultimate backup.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:40.640
You might have added
alerting and monitoring

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.380
with Stackdriver.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.815
Maybe all of this is being
computed at an output somewhere

00:14:44.815 --> 00:14:46.940
and then you actually want
to visualize it, or give

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:49.439
your analysts an opportunity
to do some interactive analysis

00:14:49.439 --> 00:14:50.684
after the computation.

00:14:50.684 --> 00:14:52.600
And of course, you might
add up a backup node.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.890
So let's walk through a concrete
example, where you're actually

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.670
doing satellite image analysis.

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:06.020
So, at the very top, you
have your image source.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:07.550
And what that image
source does is,

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.800
it takes images of
the Earth or whatever

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:13.070
it is, and pushes it to the
ingest node which is just a VM.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.560
Takes the data, does some
preliminary analysis on it.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:18.924
Let's say, for example,
puts it into Cloud Storage,

00:15:18.924 --> 00:15:20.840
and then writes some
metadata about that image

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:22.877
into Cloud Datastore.

00:15:22.877 --> 00:15:24.710
And it also notifies
the head node that hey,

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:26.610
we have something in the queue.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.810
The head node then goes, does
some intelligent and brief

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:32.085
analysis of the data,
puts it in the queue,

00:15:32.085 --> 00:15:33.710
and tells the compute
nodes to go, hey,

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:37.815
you can actually go and
start working on this thing.

00:15:37.815 --> 00:15:40.190
The compute nodes, of course,
can also take into account,

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.650
we have the Landsat data
on Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:45.710
which is free satellite data.

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:47.450
They take this data,
put it together,

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:48.680
and then they actually
write the output

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.260
through to the computation,
and then write the output data

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:53.379
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:53.379 --> 00:15:54.920
And then your analysts
can come back,

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:57.420
and then they can actually use
things like BigQuery or Cloud

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:02.730
Datalab to analyze the results.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:04.280
One thing that I
like to tell people,

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:06.740
when I talk about building
batch systems in Cloud,

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.531
is if you've ever built
one of these things

00:16:08.531 --> 00:16:10.940
before, you're probably used
to working in this mode.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:14.000
You have an on premise cluster
of maybe some thousands

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.277
of cores.

00:16:15.277 --> 00:16:17.360
And all your cores are
pretty much the same thing.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.390
And they are one size fits
all, because you need them

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:22.550
for everybody in
your organization.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.910
And you build a single queue.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:26.660
And everybody in
your organization

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:27.660
starts using that queue.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:28.780
Maybe they get
different priorities,

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:30.970
or you have some way of
trading off time, et cetera.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.920
But generally what happens
is that queue gets longer

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.795
and longer and longer,
and people get angrier

00:16:34.795 --> 00:16:36.144
and angrier and angrier.

00:16:36.144 --> 00:16:37.310
I've been in that situation.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:39.655
I've been at the
end of that queue.

00:16:39.655 --> 00:16:43.360
In the cloud, you don't
actually have to build that way.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.180
First of all, you don't have to
buy one size fits all machines.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.779
You can actually do that
with one of your clusters,

00:16:48.779 --> 00:16:51.070
but then you might realize
that a whole bunch of people

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:52.994
in your organization,
they actually need GPUs.

00:16:52.994 --> 00:16:54.910
So in the old world, you
would probably go out

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:56.099
and buy some GPU machines.

00:16:56.099 --> 00:16:57.640
With us, you can
probably rent them--

00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.372
you can rent them by the minute.

00:16:59.372 --> 00:17:01.580
You might realize that a
whole bunch of other people,

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.163
they need high CPU
machines, which are

00:17:03.163 --> 00:17:04.450
machines that have more CPU--

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.619
a higher CPU to memory ratio.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:10.410
And yet somebody else
needs higher mem machines--

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.770
high mem machines that have
more memory than they do--

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:16.564
a higher memory to CPU ratio
than a standard machine.

00:17:16.564 --> 00:17:17.980
And so what you
can actually do is

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.980
spin up clusters for each of
them, and give each of them

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:21.670
their own queue.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:24.250
So they're not really competing
with each other for time,

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:25.804
they're competing
with themselves.

00:17:25.804 --> 00:17:27.970
And they can choose to make
priorities and tradeoffs

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:28.990
as they need.

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:31.965
And then you control how
much they spend, essentially,

00:17:31.965 --> 00:17:33.590
by controlling the
size of the cluster.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.690
In the old world, you
used to have queues that

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.340
would just get infinitely long.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.950
In the cloud, you're going to
have lower queue times, which

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:48.640
leads to people actually
being able to iterate faster.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:51.140
And that usually, hopefully,
has meaningful business results

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:53.510
to you guys, aside from
just people who are happier

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:58.400
and there's less anger,
and less infighting.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.840
And then, each
virtual cluster has

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.130
nodes that actually
fit the workload

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:03.260
that that customer, that
internal customer of yours

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:04.479
actually needs.

00:18:04.479 --> 00:18:06.020
Rather than just
buying one size fits

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.330
all hardware which
leads, of course,

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:10.375
less wasted resources
and less costs.

00:18:10.375 --> 00:18:12.500
And of course, when somebody
doesn't need those GPU

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:14.660
machines, you can scale
them down and just

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.057
turn the whole thing off.

00:18:16.057 --> 00:18:18.140
Or somebody doesn't need
that high mem 32 cluster,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.120
you can just turn of
the cluster entirely.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:21.290
And because we
bill by the minute,

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:22.340
this makes perfect sense.

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:24.631
There's no reason why you
should be paying for machines

00:18:24.631 --> 00:18:26.270
that are sitting cold.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:29.657
And in the event that
somebody pushes a change

00:18:29.657 --> 00:18:31.490
to one of your clusters
and breaks the thing

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:32.690
because it was a
bad configuration

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:34.940
or something, that's just
their cluster that's broken,

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:35.810
not the entire--

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:39.180
not everybody in
the world together.

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.284
All right, let's talk about
some best practices on Cloud.

00:18:42.284 --> 00:18:43.200
How do you save money?

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:44.400
How do you scale?

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:47.800
And how do you
simplify your life?

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:49.500
Let's talk about
saving money first.

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.570
So, as I mentioned
a couple times,

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.410
to use preemptible
VMs if you can.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:54.510
It makes sense.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:57.570
They're 80% off, you mix
them with your regular VMs,

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:59.460
and you make good
progress that way.

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:00.906
Use custom machine types.

00:19:00.906 --> 00:19:02.280
Custom machine
types is something

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:05.160
that I think was actually
mentioned by Urs in his talk

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.110
earlier in the keynote.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:08.640
Essentially,
basically, it lets you

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.609
do what you see up in that
upper left corner over there--

00:19:11.609 --> 00:19:12.900
excuse me, upper right corner--

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.880
where you can actually select
how many cores and how many RAM

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.140
you want in a single machine.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.820
You don't have to buy
machines as power of two--

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:18.360
powers of 2.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:21.480
If you need 14 cores,
or you need 12 cores,

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:23.570
you can actually just set
the sliders to 14 cores

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:25.945
and set the corresponding
memory slider to what you need.

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:31.630
And that lets you fit the
actual machine to the job,

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:34.795
not try to fit the
job onto the machine.

00:19:34.795 --> 00:19:36.670
Another cool feature
that we have is instance

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:38.200
write sensing
recommendations, and that's

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:39.310
what you see over here.

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:41.649
You see those little yellow
light bulbs, I guess?

00:19:41.649 --> 00:19:43.523
That's basically where
we look at the machine

00:19:43.523 --> 00:19:46.452
and go, like, hey, this thing
is being under utilized.

00:19:46.452 --> 00:19:48.160
You have a very large
machine, but you're

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:50.020
using very little actual--

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.850
you're not using all 14
cores that you selected.

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:55.600
So maybe you just turn it
down and save some money.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.230
So this is just a very brief
overview of all your options.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:04.164
For those of you who chose to
come here, thank you very much.

00:20:04.164 --> 00:20:05.580
At this moment,
right now, there's

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.880
a parallel session going
on, "Want to Save money?

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.440
Saving money on Compute Engine."

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.110
So please find the recording of
it afterwards and check it out

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.235
if you want more details
on all the various options

00:20:15.235 --> 00:20:17.330
that we have.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:21.814
All right, now, simplifying your
life and making you happier.

00:20:21.814 --> 00:20:24.480
Obviously, Google Compute Engine
is infrastructure as a service.

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:27.360
We provide raw VMs
and networking,

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.980
but we also provide some managed
infrastructure services to make

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:32.260
your life a little bit easier.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:33.660
So for instance, auto scaling.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:36.090
You don't actually have to
build your own auto scaler.

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.440
We allow you to define
groups of instances.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.550
And then apply one of
our existing auto scalers

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:43.440
technologies to it, so that we
actually do the scaling for you

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:45.610
based on the rules
that you specify.

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:46.680
Auto healing?

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:47.610
Similar idea.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:49.710
You define what it means
for an app to be healthy.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:54.084
We take a look at it,
and if it's not healthy,

00:20:54.084 --> 00:20:55.500
we kill it and
start it over again

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.975
based on the instance group.

00:20:57.975 --> 00:20:59.350
But this, by the
way, is actually

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:00.760
very useful with
preemptible VMs,

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:03.664
because a machine that's
preempted is not healthy.

00:21:03.664 --> 00:21:05.080
And if you have a
managed instance

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.700
group of preemptible
VMs, and you

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:10.510
say I want this group to stay
at 100 nodes, and one of them

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.180
gets preempted, this
thing will automatically

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:14.390
detect and excrete a new one.

00:21:17.112 --> 00:21:18.570
And of course,
there's also rolling

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:20.760
updates and other goodness.

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:22.309
And also going on
at the same time

00:21:22.309 --> 00:21:23.850
is a talk on managed
instance groups,

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:28.950
so if you guys are interested,
please do check out that video.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:30.990
This is, I believe
my last slide,

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.060
scaling on Compute Engine.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.857
This is also sort of
in spirit of do not--

00:21:35.857 --> 00:21:38.190
or, as much as you can, try
to avoid building this stuff

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:38.690
yourself.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:40.360
It gets complicated pretty fast.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:42.830
We have a great solution,
which is essentially

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:47.040
a tutorial on our website called
HTCondor scaling on Google

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.020
Cloud with HTCondor.

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:50.730
Basically, search for
HTCondor on Google

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:54.450
Cloud, and you will find a
tutorial and a solution that

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.190
teaches you how to
use HTCondor which

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:59.910
is a batch management, open
source application that

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:03.720
lets you build batch solutions
on Google Cloud relatively

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:04.500
straightforward.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:07.787
And it takes advantage of
preemptible VMs as well.

00:22:07.787 --> 00:22:09.370
All right, that's
what I have, and I'm

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.820
going to turn it over to Bert
to talk about the goodness done

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:13.396
at Fermilab.

00:22:13.396 --> 00:22:14.770
BURT HOLZMAN:
Thank you, Michael.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:15.811
Everybody can hear me OK?

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.474
OK, so I'll talk about how
we provisioned 160,000 cores

00:22:23.474 --> 00:22:24.640
with what we call HEP cloud.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:26.620
HEP is jargon for
High Energy Physics.

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:29.800
And actually, before we
even get into physics,

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:30.730
I should mention--

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:32.590
I have to do the legal
boiler plate here.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.440
So, I work for a
government lab, so I'm not

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:38.240
giving any specific endorsement
to Google even though I'm

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:39.640
at a Google conference.

00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:41.050
I can't endorse Cloud products.

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:43.870
I can't endorse clothing
lines at Nordstrom's.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.420
You get the idea.

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:50.450
OK, so, we're trying to answer--

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:53.467
oh, hold on.

00:22:53.467 --> 00:22:55.550
You're not reading this
in Morgan Freeman's voice.

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:59.139
We're trying to answer basic
questions about the universe.

00:22:59.139 --> 00:23:00.430
So what's the universe made of?

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:04.170
How did it form, and basically
what's the past, present,

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:05.900
and future of the universe?

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:08.270
And the physics of
high energy physics--

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:10.280
the field of high
energy physics, this

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:11.910
is what we're aiming to answer.

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.360
So, you know, everything--

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:16.330
everything on this
planet is, you see it.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:18.200
It's actually here in
this periodic table.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.760
You all had high school
chemistry, I hope.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.550
And you can see
the blue line that

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.690
covers what things are made
of out to the atomic level.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:28.460
But atoms are also
made of things,

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:31.640
and we'll dive down deeper
into what those are made of.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:35.030
And so, this periodic table
was invented in, actually,

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:37.320
the 19th century.

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:41.880
And this is the periodic
table for the present term.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.275
Quarks, leptons, these are
things that we're doing.

00:23:44.275 --> 00:23:46.400
These are things we're
investigating in high energy

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:47.180
physics.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.950
And actually ordinary
matter, the quarks,

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:53.090
the up and down quark
there, and the glueon,

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:55.911
and the electron, that's all
your ordinary matter here too.

00:23:55.911 --> 00:23:58.160
And in the center is the
Higgs boson, which I hope you

00:23:58.160 --> 00:23:59.840
all heard about a
couple of years ago.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:04.000
And I'll come back and
discuss that in a bit.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.460
Fermilab, so, what is Fermilab?

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:08.800
We are the United
States' premier lab

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:11.590
for studying this field
of high energy physics.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:14.230
It always looks like this, even
though we're 40 miles outside

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:15.820
of Chicago.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:17.080
Right?

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:19.770
And we do completely
unclassified research.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:21.170
The site's open to the public.

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:23.710
We're the only national
lab that I believe

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:26.470
has zero classified research.

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:27.370
There's my office.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:31.187
Not the whole building, but
somewhere on the ninth floor.

00:24:31.187 --> 00:24:32.770
[AUDIO OUT] Fermilab,
what do we have?

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:36.070
So we have a decent size
facility on premises.

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:38.380
We've got almost 50,000 cores.

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:43.060
We've got a tape capacity
of 100 petabytes.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.190
We've got 35 petabytes of disk.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.560
And we have pretty good
network connectivity

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.140
of a multiple of 100 gigabit.

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.920
So we have pretty good
resources on site.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.020
And we're also-- I also
work for the government,

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:57.360
so we know how to--

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:01.030
and I work for science, so we
do this as cheaply as we can.

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:03.790
And probably pay people
as cheaply as we can,

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:06.730
unfortunately.

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.750
So, I should mention, we're also
the largest computing facility

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:12.190
outside of Geneva--

00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:16.386
outside of CERN in Geneva
for high energy physics.

00:25:16.386 --> 00:25:18.010
And to do high energy
physics, to probe

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:19.480
these extremely
small scales, you

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:21.640
need extremely big machines.

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:23.020
This is the Large
Hadron Collider

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:24.770
in Geneva, Switzerland.

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:29.120
The ring is about
17 miles around.

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:31.810
And to give you an
idea, if it was here,

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.580
if this is centered
here, it almost

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.290
stretches out to the Presidio.

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:38.020
And so, it's a big machine.

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:40.764
And I'm going to talk about
one of the experiments.

00:25:40.764 --> 00:25:41.680
These are underground.

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:43.660
There's a couple of
interaction points.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:46.210
I'm going to be talking about
CMS, so one of the experiments

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:49.330
at the Large Hadron Collider.

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:49.990
This is CMS.

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:51.573
That's not me, but
to give you a sense

00:25:51.573 --> 00:25:53.560
of scale of the experiment.

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:54.560
You know, these are big.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:55.660
It's a big ring.

00:25:55.660 --> 00:26:01.030
It's forming extremely tiny
things, and so it's strange,

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.280
but you need something
extremely big to detect that.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:11.460
And also, the CMS experiment--
this is the CMS experiment.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.440
It stands for Compact
Muon Solenoid.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:16.980
I know, compact seems
strange in this context.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:17.720
What happens?

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:21.556
So, we have protons colliding
14 million times per second,

00:26:21.556 --> 00:26:23.430
and this is essentially
a really large camera

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:25.560
that takes pictures.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:29.580
And we keep-- at about a
kilohertz, so 1,000 times

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:31.860
per second, we keep this data.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.410
And all measurements of a
proton-proton collision, we

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:35.119
call it an event.

00:26:35.119 --> 00:26:36.785
So it's a little
different from an event

00:26:36.785 --> 00:26:39.420
I heard about earlier, where we
talk about publishing to queues

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:40.362
and so on.

00:26:40.362 --> 00:26:41.070
These are events.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:43.830
They have [INAUDIBLE] content.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.530
And this is a very
complicated beast,

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.770
so we need simulations to
understand what we're doing.

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:52.950
And so we have simulations
that go from a stage

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:56.430
where we're basically rolling
the dice and doing random--

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:59.090
simulating events with
Monte Carlo simulation,

00:26:59.090 --> 00:27:03.420
through simulating the passage
of particles through matter

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:05.940
and how they radiate, and
how our detector responds,

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:09.042
and in the end, reconstructing
physics quantities.

00:27:09.042 --> 00:27:11.250
Not only is the detector
complicated, quantum physics

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:12.480
is in our way, too.

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:15.510
So we can't control our
initial conditions precisely.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:18.660
And we're only really
sampling a part of the space.

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:21.750
And so we need to use these
probabilistic techniques

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:24.270
to sample these spaces.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:27.090
And when we analyze this,
we compare our simulation

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.130
and extract physics results.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:32.140
Let me see if I can get
the movie going here,

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:36.138
so you can see a--

00:27:36.138 --> 00:27:38.570
this will distract
you while I talk.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:41.642
So, here's a plot
of the Higgs boson.

00:27:41.642 --> 00:27:43.100
Well, it's not the
Higgs boson yet.

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:46.460
What you see is, the black
dots are data filling in.

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.950
The blue is a simulation
of the standard model.

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:50.810
That's everything
except the Higgs

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:53.930
boson in that new periodic
table I showed you.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:56.750
And the red curve
corresponds to a new theory,

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:58.820
in this case it's
the Higgs boson.

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:01.310
And what you see,
as it fills in,

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:03.290
is you can see the Higgs
boson start to emerge.

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:06.170
Not just in our simulation, but
you can also see in the data,

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:07.700
and the error bars
will get smaller

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:10.310
as more and more data
gets accumulated.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:13.520
So, these simulations are
critical of particle physics.

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:14.720
And we're statistics driven.

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:16.470
We need a lot of them.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:17.720
We need a huge amount of data.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:19.300
Oh, I have to move
this back over.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:24.230
So the scale of
simulations we need.

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:27.050
We're simulating on the order
of billions and billions--

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:28.250
I sound like Carl Sagan--

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:31.474
we're simulating a lot
of collisions per year.

00:28:31.474 --> 00:28:33.890
These experiments at the Large
Hadron Collider are needed.

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:35.252
And workflows are--

00:28:35.252 --> 00:28:37.210
I'm going to use a
different term than Michael,

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:38.195
they're not
embarrassingly parallel.

00:28:38.195 --> 00:28:39.440
They're pleasingly parallel.

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:41.814
We have the benefit of having
these really weakly coupled

00:28:41.814 --> 00:28:44.360
things, we can run in batch
and do it efficiently.

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:48.350
So every event that we
simulate, every collision

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:52.820
can be done separately on
its own core in parallel.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:54.790
And so we need a lot
of compute to do this.

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.890
At Fermilab, not everything
is devoted to CMS.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:28:59.830
There's 70 data centers
throughout the world.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:01.496
We've been doing
this-- we've been doing

00:29:01.496 --> 00:29:06.040
this computing in this field
since the dawn of computing,

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:08.260
really, and driven
it for a long time.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:10.070
So we've been doing
this particular--

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.140
the LHC turned on less
than a decade ago.

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:17.530
And so we have 70 data
centers throughout the world.

00:29:17.530 --> 00:29:19.840
These are universities,
these are national labs,

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:22.420
these are international
laboratories, CERN and labs

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:24.844
all throughout the world.

00:29:24.844 --> 00:29:26.260
The tale of the
tape here, there's

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:30.250
about 150,000 cores worldwide
available to this experiment.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:33.010
75 petabytes of spinning
disk, 100 petabytes

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:35.122
of use tape space.

00:29:35.122 --> 00:29:37.330
And we have strong networks
connecting all the sites,

00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:40.350
not just at Fermilab, so we can
transfer data back and forth.

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:44.290
We move about five
petabytes back and forth

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:46.960
across all the sites every week.

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.170
And across the Atlantic, we
also have 340 gigabits a second.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:52.240
That's one of the things
we exercised early on.

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:55.630
Make sure we get the data
from Geneva to Fermilab

00:29:55.630 --> 00:29:57.700
as quickly as we could.

00:29:57.700 --> 00:29:58.510
That's not enough.

00:29:58.510 --> 00:30:00.343
That's a lot of compute,
but it's not enough

00:30:00.343 --> 00:30:01.690
and I'll show you why.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:02.380
You are here.

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:04.090
We're in the middle
of run two, where

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:08.060
we've got about 158 petabytes
of data for the experiment.

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:12.180
But we have an upgrade coming
in the next five to 10 years,

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:17.050
where we need estimate or
1,920 exabytes of data.

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:18.820
That's a-- petabytes is big.

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:19.969
This is ridiculously huge.

00:30:19.969 --> 00:30:22.510
So we're going to need a lot of
compute to chew through that.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:25.126
We need to do it
cheaply, because we're--

00:30:25.126 --> 00:30:26.500
well, we work for
the government,

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.050
and we're trying to get every
bang for our buck that we can

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.430
without raising your taxes.

00:30:31.430 --> 00:30:34.450
The other interesting thing
for us as a field is that,

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.820
when I was asked
about this, five, 10--

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:39.297
five years ago about Cloud
computing, the costs were--

00:30:39.297 --> 00:30:41.380
the costs were just too
high, because like I said,

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:42.460
we buy cheap.

00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:46.060
We bid, we do things
on a shoestring.

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:47.350
But the cost has come down.

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:49.750
Here is just a plot of
the costs over time,

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:51.910
and they're monotonically
decreasing to the point

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:56.320
where this becomes attractive
for us to use commercial cloud.

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:57.430
So, a challenge.

00:30:57.430 --> 00:30:59.470
So we got a--

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:03.370
through a wonderful coincidence
of ex-Fermi people working

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:05.920
at Google and the right people
being in the right pipeline

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:06.911
at the wrong time--

00:31:06.911 --> 00:31:07.660
at the wrong time?

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:09.580
At the right time.

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:12.250
Well, we attempted--
so we asked ourselves,

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:15.420
can we double the reach of
this experiment's compute?

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:18.370
Let's do a live demo,
because, you know, why not?

00:31:18.370 --> 00:31:20.440
And we'll do it during
Supercomputing, which is

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:21.890
a conference back in November.

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:25.660
Four days, 12 hours a day while
the exhibit floor is open.

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:29.140
And let's do it by expanding
Fermilab using something called

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:32.169
HEP Cloud, to 160,000 cores.

00:31:32.169 --> 00:31:34.210
And let's do it so the
application doesn't really

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:35.110
notice.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:37.030
So what's HEP Cloud?

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:38.320
And I didn't name this.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:41.410
This is named by the head
of the Office of Science.

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:45.850
But the idea is, it's basically
a portal for our researchers,

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:48.730
where we decide when--
we decide where we're

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:51.327
going to send their workflows.

00:31:51.327 --> 00:31:53.410
Whether we're going to
keep them in-house on-prem.

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:56.560
Whether we're to send them
to the grid, to Google Cloud,

00:31:56.560 --> 00:32:01.180
to the other cloud up north that
I probably shouldn't mention,

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:02.980
to our supercomputing partners.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:06.130
We have facilities at
Argonne Lab, Oak Ridge lab,

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:08.620
at Berkeley, at NERSC.

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:10.360
And so we started
a pilot project

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:12.850
to explore our
capabilities, collaborating

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:14.560
with industry and academia.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:15.791
And the goal is to move--

00:32:15.791 --> 00:32:17.290
well, I was given
the guideline, you

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.380
should get this into
production in fiscal year 18.

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.230
And so the last day of fiscal
year 18 is September 30th.

00:32:23.230 --> 00:32:26.190
So I'm going to get it
in by September 30, 2018.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:28.274
So we're in the R&amp;D phase.

00:32:28.274 --> 00:32:30.190
And the architecture--
I don't have a pointer,

00:32:30.190 --> 00:32:32.740
so I'm not going to go through
this in tremendous detail.

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:34.930
But on the left of
the dashed line,

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:37.380
we have users
submitting workflows.

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:40.240
That comes into our facility,
and we have a decision engine.

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:42.250
And the decision
engine decides when

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:45.100
we're going to provision based
on workflow characteristics,

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:50.350
based on the pricing, what
the preemptibles are going for

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:52.510
compared to what it
cost us on-prem, what's

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:54.130
available on the
grid, and so on.

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:55.652
And then we either
send it on-prem,

00:32:55.652 --> 00:32:57.610
or we have a provisioner
that goes out and gets

00:32:57.610 --> 00:32:59.680
all these different
classes of resources,

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:02.960
depending on what makes sense.

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:04.461
And in terms of
the implementation,

00:33:04.461 --> 00:33:05.210
how do we do this?

00:33:05.210 --> 00:33:08.550
We have a technology was
called the glideinWMS.

00:33:08.550 --> 00:33:11.120
Talked about HTCondor a
little bit in the last call.

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:13.100
We use Condor for two things.

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:14.560
We use it to
provision resources.

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:16.574
We go ahead and send in a pilot.

00:33:16.574 --> 00:33:18.740
We have-- and it's a whole
completely different talk

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:22.580
why pilot based workflow
management is a good idea.

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:24.110
And we don't have
time for it here.

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:26.750
But essentially, we launch
pilots on the workflows.

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:29.000
It goes in and pulls workflows--

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:33.630
jobs out of the queue, and it
helps validate environment.

00:33:33.630 --> 00:33:36.140
It manages the fact these
resources may look different.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:39.440
And to the users, it can
look like a common pool.

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.990
Even at the facility, it'll
look like a common pool.

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:44.420
And we use Condor
to accomplish this.

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:47.120
All right, so let's
look a little closer

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:48.800
at the architecture
of what we're

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:50.060
doing inside a single zone.

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:55.280
So we have a provisioner talking
to the Google Cloud APIs.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:58.834
And then we go ahead and that
launches our worker nodes.

00:33:58.834 --> 00:34:00.500
We did everything
with preemptively VMs,

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:06.370
because they're priced right for
us, and there's no critical--

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:08.120
we have enough default
tolerance built in.

00:34:08.120 --> 00:34:10.380
If something gets preempted,
we just resubmit the job.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:13.010
There's no magic state we have
to keep with these things,

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:15.710
because every collision
is independent.

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:19.400
We use-- and I'll talk about
the details of this in a bit--

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:23.570
it calls back to our head node
for our overlay batch system.

00:34:23.570 --> 00:34:26.630
It sent data back out to
Fermilab to the object store.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:29.870
We ran our input data
from Google Cloud Storage,

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:32.090
and we also had a--

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:34.040
we also need to get the
application software

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:38.719
to Google in a way that the
end user doesn't notice.

00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:43.520
And so we used some web caches
to provide the application

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:46.370
software as well as job
by job calibration data,

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:50.600
caching later in front
of our off site DB.

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:51.940
We didn't just use one zone.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:53.356
If we're going to
scale that high,

00:34:53.356 --> 00:34:56.300
it's advisable to
do more for preempt.

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.120
So we covered four
zones in central-1.

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:00.980
You can see the
architecture overall,

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:03.650
and we were reading everything
from a regional bucket

00:35:03.650 --> 00:35:08.380
from our input data
displayed in the middle.

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:11.110
OK, so to break apart
some of the pieces here.

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:13.770
So, HTCondor, this is a
batch system, but also

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:14.790
our provisioner.

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:16.290
It speaks Cloud API.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:19.985
It's initially written-- it's
written by the University

00:35:19.985 --> 00:35:22.260
of Wisconsin Madison team.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:23.947
It's been around
for a long time.

00:35:23.947 --> 00:35:25.530
And that's where
Google helped us out.

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:27.404
They contributed to this
open source project.

00:35:27.404 --> 00:35:30.686
They added support for this
to speak preemptible VMs,

00:35:30.686 --> 00:35:31.560
and service accounts.

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:33.435
And actually, they fixed
a critical bug which

00:35:33.435 --> 00:35:36.120
we found the Friday
before we were going

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:38.644
to get the demo on Monday,
to address scaling, where we

00:35:38.644 --> 00:35:40.560
couldn't get beyond--
we couldn't get anywhere

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:44.097
close to where we needed to be.

00:35:44.097 --> 00:35:46.180
How do we provide the
software to the application?

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:52.450
This is maybe a field
specific technology,

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:55.194
but essentially we have
something called CERN VMFS.

00:35:55.194 --> 00:35:57.610
It's named after CERN, the
laboratory in Switzerland where

00:35:57.610 --> 00:35:58.840
it was developed.

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:02.710
It's basically a web CDN,
the distributor application

00:36:02.710 --> 00:36:03.550
software.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:06.100
And the idea being that we
have terabytes of software

00:36:06.100 --> 00:36:07.750
sitting out there on a disk--

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:08.860
on disks.

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:11.990
We go ahead and just cache
the bits that we need.

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:13.370
It's very efficient.

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:15.910
We need-- and it also, a
lot, gives us the flexibility

00:36:15.910 --> 00:36:19.450
instead of sending a single
application into the cloud,

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:23.200
we can have many, many different
versions provided to our end

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:26.500
users, doing the--

00:36:26.500 --> 00:36:28.750
sending the
scientific workflows.

00:36:28.750 --> 00:36:31.060
And they don't know
about any of this.

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:34.240
This is completely
transparent to the user.

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:41.320
And so we had a managed instance
group, doing squid web caching.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:43.180
If there's anything
that we know,

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:45.610
cloud providers are terrific
at, and Google's terrific at,

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:46.900
its web, right?

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:50.200
So, it's an internal facing
cache using the IOBs.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:53.780
And we set it to auto scale,
probably conservatively when

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:56.640
the rates went above of
nine megabytes per second.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:59.080
And I could give you some
war stories as I go on,

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:00.520
nothing's perfect.

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:01.420
So we had an issue.

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:02.350
These are squid's.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:05.080
And there's health checks
built in, where they go ahead

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:07.937
and they try to get a file
out of your web server.

00:37:07.937 --> 00:37:09.520
That's great for
standard web servers.

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:12.220
It's awful for squids,
because squids are proxies.

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:13.690
When you do a get
on a squid, it's

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:15.790
getting not slash
blah, blah, blah,

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:19.580
but it's getting HTTP and so on.

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:23.980
And the health checks only took
something with a leading slash.

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:26.760
So unfortunately, squid
has some internal URIs,

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:29.650
so that was our solution, or
our hack to go pull those in.

00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:34.030
So, if you try to do
this, this will help you.

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:36.390
We pre-stage our
input data to GCS.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:41.045
So we have an exper--

00:37:41.045 --> 00:37:43.170
like I said, we've been
doing distributed computing

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:44.830
for a long time
with a lot of data.

00:37:44.830 --> 00:37:47.350
So there's a lot of
field specific technology

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:47.850
that we use.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:51.170
We have experiment specific
data placement services, which

00:37:51.170 --> 00:37:54.720
schedules transfers, places
data sets, and so on.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:59.681
Just this year the technologies
we're using have speak--

00:37:59.681 --> 00:38:01.680
are starting to speak the
right cloud languages.

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:04.876
In particular, they speak--
at the moment, they speak S3,

00:38:04.876 --> 00:38:06.750
and so we're able to
use the S3 compatibility

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.470
modes to transfer data in.

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.940
And we're able to
do it at 10 gigabit.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.370
And the only reason we're
limited by 10 gigabit

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:17.220
is, our object
store, I could not

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:20.310
do third party transfers without
upgrading some bits of it.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:22.860
And we needed to get
half a petabyte in,

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:25.200
and we had enough time to do it.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:28.410
So I just let it completely
redline the nick at 10 gigabit,

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:30.240
and we let it go.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:35.010
And we access these data
using a GCS fuse on startup.

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:36.590
So we access over fuse mounts.

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:39.060
Look like, slash
whatever, whatever,

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:41.830
to the application,
which was great.

00:38:41.830 --> 00:38:44.910
And it performed very well.

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:47.340
And along the way,
not just to make sure

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:49.440
the input was working,
but the output looked

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.939
like we were going to need more
than 10 gigabit at the scale

00:38:51.939 --> 00:38:53.560
we're talking about.

00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:57.030
So thanks to Google, and also
our energy sciences network,

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:58.770
the pairing was
upgraded to 100 gigabit

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:02.730
temporarily for
our work, and then

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:04.392
ironically, it was
actually scheduled

00:39:04.392 --> 00:39:06.100
to get upgraded a
couple of months later.

00:39:06.100 --> 00:39:07.270
So it's 100 gigabit now.

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:14.310
We also converted-- we
had a regional bucket

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:16.080
for Cloud Storage.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:18.240
We converted that
overnight to save costs.

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:19.990
As we want to realize
that we were fine,

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.090
we had enough capacity
in a single region.

00:39:22.090 --> 00:39:25.170
Overnight, half a
petabyte was converted,

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:26.660
and it cost us 30% less.

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:33.370
So, right, there's
big question, right?

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:35.560
I talked all this, gave
you all the architecture.

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:38.000
Question is, how do we do?

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:38.530
We do great!

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:40.190
So, this is the
cores from Google.

00:39:40.190 --> 00:39:42.805
This is a plot from
our own dashboards.

00:39:42.805 --> 00:39:44.657
They're running at CERN.

00:39:44.657 --> 00:39:46.240
And the different
colors you see here,

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:48.390
everything else-- everything
that's not purple,

00:39:48.390 --> 00:39:49.510
are different sites.

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:52.990
The red is-- the red,
which is, let's see.

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:55.690
I don't have a pointer,
but the red that's on top,

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:56.826
that's Fermilab.

00:39:56.826 --> 00:39:58.450
And everything else,
this is everything

00:39:58.450 --> 00:40:01.010
we run all over the
world during this time.

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:04.210
And what you see is, we
doubled the computing capacity

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:05.190
for the experiment.

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:08.800
300,000 cores contributing to
science, to high energy physics

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:10.150
during this time.

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.380
And here's a plot
from our dashboards.

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:15.280
We're ingesting
this into Grafana.

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:17.050
And the money plot--

00:40:17.050 --> 00:40:19.450
I have other plots at
160,000, but there's

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:20.824
one I took a screenshot of.

00:40:20.824 --> 00:40:22.990
The money plot is the one
all the way in the middle.

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:27.220
The pie chart, the
yellow is everything

00:40:27.220 --> 00:40:28.720
that we have all
over the world, all

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:34.050
these 70 data centers running,
in teraflops per second--

00:40:34.050 --> 00:40:34.900
in teraflops.

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:38.650
And the green is what
we're getting from GCE.

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:41.800
So we were able
to hit our target.

00:40:41.800 --> 00:40:45.820
And I should say, we
were able to do this in--

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:48.520
we got the go ahead to
do this three and 1/2

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:51.820
weeks before the conference,
before the Supercomputing.

00:40:51.820 --> 00:40:54.970
So we were able to do it in
record time, thanks also--

00:40:54.970 --> 00:40:58.270
thanks to Fermilab, and
also a great team at Google.

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:00.600
All right, so what are
some lessons we learned?

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:03.277
Michael talked about the
custom machine types.

00:41:03.277 --> 00:41:05.110
We started out with a
standard machine type.

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:06.490
That's more than we needed.

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:09.670
We trimmed it down and it
was extremely easy to do so.

00:41:09.670 --> 00:41:12.100
And there was a
20% cost savings.

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:15.777
I mentioned the code that
Google helped us fix.

00:41:15.777 --> 00:41:17.860
We had a bug in our-- there
was a bug in the code.

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:19.150
It's been there
ever since Condor

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:20.980
put in support for
GCE, which has probably

00:41:20.980 --> 00:41:23.680
been three, four, five years.

00:41:23.680 --> 00:41:28.360
It ignored the AP pagination
API, which, it really shows up

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:31.060
in funny ways because you get
an alphabetized list of VMs that

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:34.660
were getting deleted out from
under us, because our state,

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:36.940
we had no idea
what our state was.

00:41:36.940 --> 00:41:38.890
Google provided the patch.

00:41:38.890 --> 00:41:41.590
And then, here's a fun
one, as we were expanding

00:41:41.590 --> 00:41:44.020
capacity in the
zone, in the region,

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:47.244
we went from 4k to 16k IPs.

00:41:47.244 --> 00:41:48.910
There should be a
carriage return there,

00:41:48.910 --> 00:41:51.340
but it's a simple API command,
Gcloud, Compute Network

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:53.830
Subnets, Expand IP
range, and so on.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:57.310
That's great, except maybe you
see the problem in that red 20

00:41:57.310 --> 00:41:59.670
right there in the firewall?

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:01.270
Yeah, 20 is 4k--

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:04.270
20 bits is 4k, so
we ran into that.

00:42:04.270 --> 00:42:05.976
We fixed it, but
when you go to scale,

00:42:05.976 --> 00:42:07.600
these are sorts of
things you discover.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:10.090
We would have never
discovered this pagination bug

00:42:10.090 --> 00:42:15.390
if we had kept it 10,000
cores or something small.

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:17.780
All right, so here are some
of the numbers, the tale

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:19.330
of the tape so to speak.

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:22.780
We ran about six-- almost six
and 1/2 million hours of wall

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:24.940
time on these resources.

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:27.310
We had five and 1/2
for completed jobs.

00:42:27.310 --> 00:42:30.580
We submitted more than 730,000
of these simulation jobs.

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:32.290
And each job is more
than one collision.

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:33.880
It's a whole host of them.

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:36.460
Only 47 didn't complete,
because we have fault tolerance

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:40.030
with our batch, we have fault
tolerance with the application

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:42.850
that feeds the batch.

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:46.594
And once we got out of
the infant mortality

00:42:46.594 --> 00:42:48.010
of the first day
of the demo, when

00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:50.830
we found some of these issues
like that firewall issue

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:53.890
was day one, where
we hurriedly, where

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:56.020
we spent a lot of time
hacking at laptops to get

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:57.165
to the bottom of that.

00:42:57.165 --> 00:43:00.280
The good put, in other
words, which is--

00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:01.400
it was at 94%.

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:04.570
In other words, 6% of
the cycles that we had

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:07.330
were wasted in results
we didn't get back.

00:43:07.330 --> 00:43:09.580
94% of our compute
came back to us

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:13.540
even though we were using
exclusively preemptible VMs.

00:43:13.540 --> 00:43:15.700
So we had a terrific
result. That's

00:43:15.700 --> 00:43:17.260
better than a lot
of our grid sites,

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:19.790
which are not
necessarily dependable.

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:21.549
Well, it depends which
university and lab

00:43:21.549 --> 00:43:22.090
are going to.

00:43:22.090 --> 00:43:23.410
It's a host of sites.

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:27.040
But that's better than a lot
of our grid computing sites.

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:30.910
I'd be fired if it was better
than Fermilab probably,

00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:32.890
but-- so, we do--

00:43:32.890 --> 00:43:36.280
well, it depends on what
the jobs we're doing, too.

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.590
In terms of the cost, so,
if you were the cost--

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:40.840
this is what it cost us
during Supercomputing.

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:42.940
The main cost was,
not surprisingly,

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:44.870
the cost to compute for the VMs.

00:43:44.870 --> 00:43:47.940
It cost us about $8.5k
for network egress.

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:50.920
You pay for every byte you
get out of the network.

00:43:50.920 --> 00:43:53.770
We optimized our workflow, so
that didn't bite us too much.

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:55.900
There are other workflows
that will cost more.

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:57.400
The disk that's
attached to the VMs

00:43:57.400 --> 00:43:59.380
that we needed
for our workflows.

00:43:59.380 --> 00:44:02.530
And then the storage, that
half a petabyte that we stored,

00:44:02.530 --> 00:44:04.720
cost about $3,500.

00:44:04.720 --> 00:44:07.170
We generate 200
million physics events.

00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:09.590
81 terabytes of data
for the experiment.

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:11.080
And in terms of the cost--

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:13.960
and these costs have a lot
of caveats built in, so--

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:17.680
it ended up costing us
about $0.016 per core hour.

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:20.830
On premises, our costs are
about a little less than $0.01

00:44:20.830 --> 00:44:23.470
cent per core hour, but that
makes a bunch of assumptions.

00:44:23.470 --> 00:44:26.110
Number one, that
we're 100% utilized,

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:27.850
and since I work
for Fermilab I wont

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:31.780
show any plots demonstrating
anything different.

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:34.267
It also-- keep in mind I
work, as I've said many times,

00:44:34.267 --> 00:44:35.350
I work for the government.

00:44:35.350 --> 00:44:38.770
And Fermilab being this high
energy physics frontier,

00:44:38.770 --> 00:44:42.000
we have a collider at Fermilab.

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:43.390
It's a big deal.

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:46.990
And we get power for
cheap, because it's a lot--

00:44:46.990 --> 00:44:48.580
believe me, it may
have cost you a lot

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:50.560
to run power to your
datacenter, but running

00:44:50.560 --> 00:44:54.322
a proton-proton collider
is quite a lot of power.

00:44:54.322 --> 00:44:55.780
So we get a good
rate on our power.

00:44:58.282 --> 00:45:00.740
So we did this for CMS, for
this experiment I talked about,

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:02.900
the Large Hadron Collider.

00:45:02.900 --> 00:45:05.270
We had some credits left over.

00:45:05.270 --> 00:45:06.920
We had some impetus
to see what we

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:08.540
could do after Supercomputing.

00:45:08.540 --> 00:45:10.460
So how quickly could
we on-board a new user?

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:12.770
Somebody that hasn't
done anything with GCE,

00:45:12.770 --> 00:45:14.600
the CMS was part of this run up.

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:16.250
So there's an
experiment called Mu2e.

00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:18.500
I won't go into the
physics in any detail,

00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:21.710
but the basic idea is trying to
measure the rare decay of muons

00:45:21.710 --> 00:45:24.800
to electrons which has
never been observed.

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:26.570
And so it would be
a complete change

00:45:26.570 --> 00:45:29.680
to the standard model
of particle physics

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:31.070
and be really interesting.

00:45:31.070 --> 00:45:32.905
The experiment's
getting designed now,

00:45:32.905 --> 00:45:35.030
so it's in the design stage
of trying to figure out

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:36.080
where to put all of these bits?

00:45:36.080 --> 00:45:37.901
And I want to talk
about the technical bits

00:45:37.901 --> 00:45:40.400
of what these things are, but
where do you put the detectors

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:42.026
and optimize them?

00:45:42.026 --> 00:45:43.400
So that's where
we're simulating.

00:45:43.400 --> 00:45:45.400
Different placement,
different geometry, how big

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:47.270
should things be
for this detector?

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:49.190
So, how quickly do
we get them on board?

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:50.810
Less than a day.

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:51.920
They were ready for--

00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:53.420
now, this is an
experiment that knew

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:56.120
how to do grid computing,
distributed computing.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:57.722
But we had all the
tools in place.

00:45:57.722 --> 00:45:59.180
They were prepared--
and it took us

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:01.370
less than a day
after Supercomputing

00:46:01.370 --> 00:46:02.630
to get them up on GCE.

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:04.941
And what you see here is,
the green is Google Cloud,

00:46:04.941 --> 00:46:06.440
and the yellow is
what we're running

00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:08.760
for them on-prem at Fermi lab.

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:14.090
So, that's a terrific
result, I think,

00:46:14.090 --> 00:46:19.550
and it shows that
our field actually,

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:22.490
having being prepared for
distributed computing,

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:23.990
understands what they need--

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:25.406
understand some
of the things they

00:46:25.406 --> 00:46:28.650
need to do to be able to
bring stuff into the cloud.

00:46:28.650 --> 00:46:31.090
All right, so what
are we doing next?

00:46:31.090 --> 00:46:33.790
Like I said, we're moving to
[INAUDIBLE] in September 2018.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:36.539
We have this decision engine
component, which is, I think,

00:46:36.539 --> 00:46:37.330
really interesting.

00:46:37.330 --> 00:46:39.280
The heart of it, it's
also extremely difficult.

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:41.363
And I don't need to get
anything optimal in there,

00:46:41.363 --> 00:46:43.190
but that's in R&amp;D.

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:45.590
Our super-- the
Department of Energy,

00:46:45.590 --> 00:46:47.890
and again this is your
tax dollars at work,

00:46:47.890 --> 00:46:50.880
but we have supercomputers,
HPC facilities.

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:53.470
And these are becoming
much more friendly for data

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:54.490
intensive workflows.

00:46:54.490 --> 00:46:58.210
And so, we're provisioning
cycles already at Berkeley,

00:46:58.210 --> 00:47:01.734
at NERSC, on Edison and Cory,
and we're expanding this.

00:47:01.734 --> 00:47:03.650
There's additional
commercial cloud providers.

00:47:03.650 --> 00:47:04.733
And I talked about Google.

00:47:04.733 --> 00:47:08.420
We've done something
similar with Amazon.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:13.210
Next, everybody next on--

00:47:13.210 --> 00:47:14.800
since I work for
the government, I

00:47:14.800 --> 00:47:17.050
have to put things out to bid.

00:47:17.050 --> 00:47:20.350
And we spread things as--
we spread the manure over

00:47:20.350 --> 00:47:23.110
as many fields as we
can get to, right?

00:47:23.110 --> 00:47:25.510
And then there's non-pleasingly
parallel problems.

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:27.940
So, you know, deep learning.

00:47:27.940 --> 00:47:30.400
There are-- there's a
lot of compute involved

00:47:30.400 --> 00:47:34.000
with doing these
simulations, and doing

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:36.280
the data reconstruction,
the reconstruction we

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:37.480
do on our data.

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:39.340
But we have other
interesting problems

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:41.298
that may lend themselves
well to deep learning.

00:47:41.298 --> 00:47:44.830
We have thousands of tons
of liquid argon detectors,

00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:46.860
where we're looked
looking for 3-D tracks.

00:47:46.860 --> 00:47:49.360
And when you start looking at
what you need to do with this,

00:47:49.360 --> 00:47:53.590
it looks an awful lot like image
classification in 3-D problems.

00:47:53.590 --> 00:47:55.540
And so the field is
actually starting

00:47:55.540 --> 00:47:59.540
to re-embrace deep learning.

00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:01.952
There is-- the
field embraced what

00:48:01.952 --> 00:48:03.160
it is called neural networks.

00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:06.459
The field got involved, but
we didn't have the compute.

00:48:06.459 --> 00:48:08.500
I think we probably had
enough data in the field,

00:48:08.500 --> 00:48:12.790
but we didn't have the
compute to really make

00:48:12.790 --> 00:48:16.330
a sea change in how
we did our analyses,

00:48:16.330 --> 00:48:18.040
and new architectures.

00:48:18.040 --> 00:48:19.660
All right, so I just
wanted to thank--

00:48:19.660 --> 00:48:21.100
this isn't just my work.

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:22.840
It's a work of cast of many.

00:48:22.840 --> 00:48:25.590
So we have a great team at
Fermilab that did the work.

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:28.439
The HTCondor and
glideinWMS projects.

00:48:28.439 --> 00:48:30.730
Open Science Grid, I didn't
talk much about this except

00:48:30.730 --> 00:48:32.756
in the concept of grid,
but a lot of the stuff

00:48:32.756 --> 00:48:34.630
that sits underneath,
the software packaging,

00:48:34.630 --> 00:48:38.430
the tooling, the CERN VMFS,
the stuff that gets packaged.

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:40.872
When things land on
our worker nodes,

00:48:40.872 --> 00:48:43.330
they have all the tools we need
from the Open Science Grid.

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:45.705
It's a collaborative project
between the National Science

00:48:45.705 --> 00:48:48.405
Foundation and the
Department of Energy.

00:48:48.405 --> 00:48:49.780
The Energy Sciences
Network, none

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:52.196
of this-- you can't do any of
this without networking data

00:48:52.196 --> 00:48:53.200
intensive science.

00:48:53.200 --> 00:48:56.650
Our team at Google, Michael,
Karan, Solomon, Sam, Paul--

00:48:56.650 --> 00:48:59.350
the Pauls, and Doug Strain.

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:00.697
And, you know, I again--

00:49:00.697 --> 00:49:01.780
I work for the government.

00:49:01.780 --> 00:49:03.738
I can't buy stuff directly
from Google, either.

00:49:03.738 --> 00:49:06.250
Your terms and conditions
are things I can't agree to.

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:08.310
So you need to go
through resellers

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:10.600
that take care of that for us.

00:49:10.600 --> 00:49:14.404
And I can't advertise
for commercial products,

00:49:14.404 --> 00:49:16.070
but I will advertise
for the laboratory.

00:49:16.070 --> 00:49:18.340
So it's our 50th
anniversary at Fermilab.

00:49:18.340 --> 00:49:20.650
If you're anywhere near
Chicago, come by this year.

00:49:20.650 --> 00:49:21.700
There's a website.

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:25.210
We've got symposia, we've got
a big birthday celebration.

00:49:25.210 --> 00:49:27.210
We're going to have an
enormous open house.

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:28.570
Campus is open to the public.

00:49:28.570 --> 00:49:29.740
Please come by.

00:49:29.740 --> 00:49:30.730
And that's it.

00:49:30.730 --> 00:49:32.370
Thank you.

